{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel.</b> Our Rabbis have already said (Torat Kohanim, beginning of the parashah) that this parashah was stated in an assembly, because most of the essential principles of the Torah depend upon it, and this is the reason for \"Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel.\" But it is mentioned here in the Torah of the Priests because it speaks in it of the shelamim offering, and because it will mention in it the judgment that He will command to carry out against those who commit the abominations listed therein. And the forbidden relations and the abominations were mentioned in this book for the reason that we wrote at the beginning of the book." "<b>You shall be holy.</b> Be separated (perushim) from forbidden relations and from sin, for everywhere you find a fence against forbidden relations you find holiness — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:2). But in Torat Kohanim (parashah 1, chapter 2) I found it stated simply: \"You shall be separated.\" And so they taught there (Shemini, chapter 12, mishnah 3): \"'And you shall sanctify yourselves and you shall be holy, for I am holy' — just as I am holy, so you shall be holy; just as I am separated, so you shall be separated.\" Now in my opinion, this separation is not separation from forbidden relations as the Rav said, but rather the separation that is mentioned everywhere in the Talmud, whose practitioners are called \"Perushim.\" The principle is as follows: the Torah forbade forbidden relations and forbidden foods, and permitted relations between a man and his wife and the eating of meat and wine. If so, a man of desire might find room to be swept up in the lust of his wife or his many wives, and to be among the wine-drinkers and the gluttons, and to speak as he pleases of all vulgarities, since this particular prohibition was not mentioned in the Torah — and behold, he would be a degenerate (naval) with the permission of the Torah. Therefore Scripture, after detailing the prohibitions that it forbade entirely, commanded with a general principle — that we be separated from what is permitted: he should diminish sexual relations, in the manner that they said (Berakhot 22a) that Torah scholars should not be frequent among their wives like roosters, and he should engage only as needed for the fulfillment of the mitzvah incumbent upon him; and he should sanctify himself from wine by drinking it sparingly, as Scripture (Numbers 6:5) called the nazirite \"holy,\" and he should remember the evils attributed to it in the Torah (Genesis 9:21) in Noah and in Lot; and likewise he should separate himself from impurity, even though we were not warned against it in the Torah, as they mentioned (Hagigah 18b) that the garments of an am ha-aretz are a source of midras-impurity for the Perushim, and as the nazirite is called \"holy\" (Numbers 6:8) for guarding even against the impurity of the dead; and he should also guard his mouth and his tongue from becoming defiled through gross overeating and repulsive speech, in the manner that Scripture mentioned (Isaiah 9:16), \"and every mouth speaks villainy,\" and he should sanctify himself in this until he reaches the level of separation (perishut), as they said about Rabbi Chiya that he never engaged in idle conversation all his days. Through these and things like them, this general mitzvah comes — after it has detailed all the transgressions that are entirely forbidden — so that he enters into the scope of this command the cleanliness of his hands and his body, as they said (Berakhot 53b): \"'And you shall sanctify yourselves' — these are the first waters; 'and you shall be holy' — these are the last waters; 'for I am holy' — this is the fragrant oil.\" For even though these are rabbinic mitzvot, the essence of Scripture in things like these warns that we be clean and pure and separated from the masses of humanity, who soil themselves with what is permitted and with the repulsive. And this is the way of the Torah: to specify and then to include in a general principle things of this sort. For after the warning against the specifics of the laws in every matter of business between people — \"you shall not steal,\" \"you shall not rob,\" \"you shall not oppress,\" and the rest of the prohibitions — it said in a general clause, \"and you shall do what is right and good\" (Deuteronomy 6:18), so that under the doing of what is right and equity and everything beyond the strict line of the law for the benefit of his fellows he includes all, as I shall explain (there) when I reach its place in the will of the Holy One, blessed be He. And so too regarding the Sabbath: it forbade labors by a negative commandment and exertions by a general positive command, as it is written, \"you shall rest\" — and I shall further explain this (Ramban on Leviticus 23:24), God willing." "And the reason Scripture said, <b>For I, YHWH your Elohim, am holy</b>, is to say that we merit cleaving to Him when we are holy, and this is similar in meaning to the first utterance of the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:2–3). And He commanded, \"You shall each fear his mother and his father\" (Leviticus 19:3), for there He commanded concerning honor and here He commands concerning awe. And He said, \"and you shall keep My Sabbaths,\" for there He commanded concerning remembrance and here concerning observance. And we have already explained (Exodus 20:8) the meaning of both of them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "Ramban indicates a passage from the Ten Commandments by citing Exodus 20:2, but parenthetically appends (ג), apparently referencing verse 3 as well; this notation is preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:13.784564369Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}